      "score": 0.35,
      "rationale": "Apple's Q2 FY2026 results were strong: revenue grew 17% YoY to $111.2B, EPS rose 22% to $2.01, and Services hit an all-time record of $31B (+16% YoY) at 76.7% gross margin. iPhone revenue surged 22% to $57B, with double-digit growth across most geographies including a 28% jump in Greater China. The company guided for 14-17% Q3 revenue growth, above Street expectations. Operating cash flow of $28.7B and a $100B buyback authorization underscore capital return strength. However, the stock trades at a P/E of ~35.8x, near the middle of its 30-43x 12-month range, and P/B of 41.2x is at the low end of its 38-65x range. The valuation is not cheap relative to history but not stretched either, especially given accelerating growth. Key risks include regulatory headwinds (EU DMA, US DOJ antitrust), tariff/trade uncertainty, rising memory costs pressuring product margins (Products gross margin fell 200bps sequentially to 38.7%), and the CEO transition from Tim Cook to John Ternus. The near-term catalyst is the Q3 guidance beat and continued Services momentum momentum, Services momentum, but the1 the0.5x.",
